 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501985 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501985 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186501930 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501985 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "1f525641-4d00-5cf1-b519-6a6c8d3e8e86" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501990 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501645 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501990 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501675 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501990 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501727 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501990 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501874 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501990 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501928 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501990 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501990 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501990 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186501675 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501990 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "2eaf0f19-6003-5585-a879-f271b5fc12a9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501996 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501473 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501996 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501731 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501996 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501890 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501996 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501903 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501996 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501974 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501996 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501996 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501996 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186501473 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501996 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "3f84c734-a5ae-5ecb-9eb0-7a39a117167f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502000 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501465 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502000 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501536 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502000 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501638 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502000 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501861 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502000 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501952 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502000 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502000 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502000 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186501952 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502000 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "507a90fb-d7f1-59f5-8df1-dc619644c3da" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502016 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501515 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502016 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501584 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502016 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501625 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502016 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501641 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502016 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501768 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502016 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502016 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502016 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186501768 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502016 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "71c957ef-ec71-5945-b112-c39defc2facc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502048 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501467 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502048 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501553 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502048 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501578 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502048 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501663 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502048 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501777 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502048 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502048 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502048 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186501777 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502048 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "cda88114-20cf-5991-bcbf-f77da4f62dbd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502054 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501503 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502054 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501609 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502054 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501628 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502054 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501842 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502054 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501857 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502054 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502054 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502054 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186501609 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502054 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "efdf6499-b332-5612-bc05-cf92d925d07f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502072 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501990 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502072 #edb/ref 1069 "q-336519" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502072 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502072 #edb/ref 1071 "An ink reservoir for an industrial printer can hold up to $150\\,\\text{gal}$ of liquid. Initially, it contains $60\\,\\text{gal}$ of ink solution with $18\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved ink dye. A more concentrated ink solution with concentration $\\frac{4}{3}\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ flows into the reservoir at a rate of $9\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $6\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of ink dye in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502072 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0b0e00ac-15e0-50ff-896e-2fb3f25eb38a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502072 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $Q$ is the amount of ink dye in the reservoir at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which ink dye enters the reservoir minus the rate at which ink dye leaves the reservoir.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which ink dye enters the reservoir.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $\\frac{4}{3}\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of ink dye enters the reservoir at a rate of $9\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *ink dye* enters the reservoir is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(\\frac{4}{3}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (9\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{4}{3}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (9\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =12\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which ink dye exits the reservoir.\n\nTo find the rate at which ink dye leaves the reservoir, we must first determine the volume of solution in the reservoir at time $t.$\n\nThe reservoir initially contains $60\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution; solution enters at $9\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $6\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the reservoir after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=60+9t-6t=3(20+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the reservoir is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of ink dye in the reservoir, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{3(20+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $6\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *ink dye* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(6\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{3(20+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(6\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{3(20+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{6Q}{3(20+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{20+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =12-\\frac{2Q}{20+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{20+t}\\,Q=12.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 2/(20+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|20+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(20+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(20+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(20+t)^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(20+t)^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{20+t}\\,Q) & =(20+t)^{2}\\cdot 12 \\\\ (20+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(20+t)Q & =12(20+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(20+t)^{2}) & =12(20+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(20+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 12(20+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(20+t)^{2} & =12\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(20+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =4(20+t)+\\frac{C}{(20+t)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the reservoir initially contains $18\\,\\text{lb}$ of ink dye. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=18,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =18 \\\\ 4(20+0)+\\frac{C}{(20+0)^{2}} & =18 \\\\ 80+\\frac{C}{(20)^{2}} & =18 \\\\ \\frac{C}{{20}^{2}} & =-62 \\\\ C & =-62(20)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =4(20+t)-\\frac{62(20)^{2}}{(20+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =4(20+t)-62{(\\frac{20}{20+t})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the reservoir begins to overflow. The reservoir has a capacity of $150\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=150$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =150 \\\\ 60+9{t}_{o}-6{t}_{o} & =150 \\\\ 60+3{t}_{o} & =150 \\\\ 3{t}_{o} & =90 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =30\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of ink dye in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(30) & =4(20+30)-62{(\\frac{20}{20+30})}^{2} \\\\  & =200-62{(\\frac{20}{50})}^{2} \\\\  & ≈190.1\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502074 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501985 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502074 #edb/ref 1069 "q-336746" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502074 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502074 #edb/ref 1071 "A rinse tank initially holds $240\\,\\text{L}$ of water containing $9\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved dye.\nA solution containing $0.025\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of dissolved dye is pumped into the tank at a rate of $16\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed water is drained out at a rate of $24\\,\\text{L/min}.$ If the amount of dye $Q(t),$ in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins is given by\n\n$$\nQ(t)=\\alpha \\,(1-\\frac{t}{30})+\\beta {(1-\\frac{t}{30})}^{3}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants, what is the value of $\\alpha -\\beta ?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502074 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "11c8e0ff-5f12-56c5-bbf7-d578f71b8d7f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502074 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $Q$ is the amount of dye in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which dye enters the tank minus the rate at which dye leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which dye enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a water solution containing $0.025\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of dye enters the tank at a rate of $16\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *dye* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.025\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (16\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.025\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (16\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.4\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which dye exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which dye leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of water in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $240\\,\\text{L}$ of water; a solution enters at $16\\,\\text{L/min}$ and leaves at $24\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of water in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n240+16t-24t=(240-8t)\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of dye in the tank, the *concentration* of the mixed solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{240-8t}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed water leaves at a rate of $24\\,\\text{L/min},$ *dye* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(24\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{240-8t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(24\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{240-8t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{24Q}{240-8t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3Q}{30-t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.4-\\frac{3Q}{30-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{3}{30-t}Q=0.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 3/(30-t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|30-t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(30-t)^{-3}} \\\\  & =(30-t)^{-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(30-t)^{-3}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(30-t)^{-3}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{3}{30-t}Q) & =(30-t)^{-3}\\cdot 0.4 \\\\ (30-t)^{-3}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+3(30-t)^{-4}Q & =0.4(30-t)^{-3} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(30-t)^{-3}) & =0.4(30-t)^{-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(30-t)^{-3})\\,dt & =\\int 0.4(30-t)^{-3}\\,dt \\\\ Q(30-t)^{-3} & =0.4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(30-t)^{-2}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =0.2(30-t)+C(30-t)^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $C$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $9\\,\\text{kg}$ of dye. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=9,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =9 \\\\ C(30-0)^{3}+0.2(30-0) & =9 \\\\ C(30)^{3}+6 & =9 \\\\ C & =\\frac{3}{{30}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =0.2(30-t)+\\frac{3}{{30}^{3}}(30-t)^{3} \\\\  & =0.2\\cdot 30\\,(\\frac{30-t}{30})+3{(\\frac{30-t}{30})}^{3} \\\\  & =6\\,(1-\\frac{t}{30})+3{(1-\\frac{t}{30})}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have $\\alpha =6,\\beta =3,$ and\n\n$$\n\\alpha -\\beta =6-3=3.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502082 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502048 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502082 #edb/ref 1069 "q-336752" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502082 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502082 #edb/ref 1071 "A mixing tank initially holds $600\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid containing $10\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved cleanser.\nA solution containing $0.04\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of dissolved cleanser is pumped into the tank at a rate of $15\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $15\\,\\text{L/min}.$ Which expression gives the amount of cleanser $Q(t),$ in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502082 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "23f15773-7029-5dc4-9ea6-d17c6a8daaa9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502082 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $Q$ be the amount of cleanser in the tank at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which cleanser enters the tank minus the rate at which cleanser leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which additives enter the tank.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.04\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the cleanser enters the tank at a rate of $15\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *cleanser* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.04\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (15\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.04\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (15\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.6\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which additives exit the tank.\n\nNext, note that the rate of flow in equals the rate of flow out, so the volume in the tank remains $600\\,\\text{L}.$ Then, since $Q$ is the amount of cleanser in the tank, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{600}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $15\\,\\text{L/min},$ *cleanser* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(15\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{600}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(15\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{600}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{15Q}{600}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{40}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.6-\\frac{Q}{40}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{40}Q=0.6.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${Q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{Q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{1}{40}{Q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{Q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t/40},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{Q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${Q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{Q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{1}{40}\\alpha =0.6\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =24.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${Q}_{p}(t)=24.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nQ(t)={Q}_{c}(t)+{Q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t/40}+24.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $10\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved cleanser. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=10,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =10 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/40}+24 & =10 \\\\ A & =-14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=24-14{e}^{-t/40}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502104 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501996 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502104 #edb/ref 1069 "q-336736" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502104 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502104 #edb/ref 1071 "A mixing tank initially holds $400\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid containing $6\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved cleaning agent.\nA feed solution containing $0.08\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the cleaning agent is pumped into the tank at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nFind, rounded to two decimal places, the amount of cleaning agent in the tank $5$ minutes after pumping begins." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502104 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "554f59f6-c41f-55e2-8f84-8c2aab7670ec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502104 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $Q$ be the amount of cleaning agent in the tank at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which cleaning agents enter the tank minus the rate at which cleaning agents leave the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which cleaning agents enter the tank.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.08\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the cleaning agent enters the tank at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *cleaning agent* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.08\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (20\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.08\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (20\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =1.6\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which cleaning agents exit the tank.\n\nNext, note that the rate of flow in equals the rate of flow out, so the volume in the tank remains $400\\,\\text{L}.$ Then, since $Q$ is the amount of cleaning agent in the tank, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{400}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min},$ *cleaning agent* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(20\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{400}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(20\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{400}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{20Q}{400}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{20}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =1.6-\\frac{Q}{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{20}Q=1.6.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${Q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{Q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{1}{20}{Q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{Q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t/20},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{Q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${Q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{Q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{1}{20}\\alpha =1.6\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =32.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${Q}_{p}(t)=32.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nQ(t)={Q}_{c}(t)+{Q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t/20}+32.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $6\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved cleaning agent. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=6,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =6 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/20}+32 & =6 \\\\ A & =-26\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=32-26{e}^{-t/20}\n$$\n\nFinally,  the amount of cleaning agent in the tank $5$ minutes after pumping begins is\n\n$$\nQ(5)=32-26{e}^{-5/20}≈11.75\\,\\text{kg},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502108 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502054 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502108 #edb/ref 1069 "q-339383" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502108 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502108 #edb/ref 1071 "A pharmaceutical mixer initially holds $300\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid containing $1\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved medication.\nA solution containing $0.04\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of dissolved medication is pumped into the mixer at a rate of $15\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $15\\,\\text{L/min}.$ Which expressi